From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 11:36:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 357/488] DeepSeek-V4.1: Add a TileLang vocabulary-parallel
 embedding lookup

The decode step's last kernels outside TileLang and Triton are vLLM's CUDA
vocabulary embedding and three cuBLAS launches (the torch-profiled decode of
image -next-2 on the ring, every rank: 26 one-stream steps, 109 sixteen-stream
steps; spark-ds41f experiments/2026-10-24-tilelang-completion). This commit adds
the TileLang lookup; the next ones add the confidence head, switch both call
sites and time each against what it replaces.

Findings

vllm::vocab_embedding::vocab_parallel_embedding_kernel<int, uint4>: 208 calls at
one stream, 872 at sixteen, two a step on each rank (2.7 and 4.5 us a call).
- Call site: VocabParallelEmbedding.forward, through ops.vocab_parallel_embedding,
  which a TP > 1 layer with the unquantized embedding method takes
  (use_fused_embedding); kernels.embedding_method() returns that method under
  the TileLang family. The two calls are the target's embed_tokens
  (DeepseekV4Model.forward -> embed_input_ids) and the DSpark drafter's
  (DSparkDeepseekV4Model.forward -> embed_input_ids; the drafter aliases the
  target's embed_tokens). Prefill SP's forward_reduce_scatter launches it too.
- Shapes: int32 ids; the rank's BF16 shard, 32320 x 5120 at TP4 (129280 / 4),
  43136 x 5120 at TP3 (129408 padded / 3); 10240-byte rows, copied as uint4.
  One stream: the target embeds 1 + up to 5 drafts (capture sizes 1-4, 6) and
  the drafter one query block of 5. Sixteen streams: up to 96 and 80 rows.
  Prefill: up to 8192 (TP4) or 4096 (TP3) tokens.
- Computes: each token's shard row when the rank owns its id, zeros otherwise
  (LoRA's added vocabulary, empty here, maps after the padded base rows). The
  all-reduce adds one row to zeros, so the lookup is exact.

void dot_kernel<float, 128, 0, cublasDotParams<cublasGemvTensorStridedBatched
<float const>, ...>> and void reduce_1Block_kernel<float, 128, 7, ...>: 104
calls each at one stream (3.0 and 1.3 us), 20 at sixteen; and
internal::gemvx::kernel<int, int, float, float, float, float, false, true, ...>:
416 calls at sixteen streams only (5.4 us). One FP32 product a draft step on
each rank: dot and its reduction at one stream; at sixteen streams the GEMV on
104 of the 109 steps and the dot pair on the other 5 (20 = 5 x 4 ranks).
- Call site: DSparkSpeculator._sample_sequential -> compute_confidence
  (models/deepseek_v4_1/nvidia/dspark.py) -> DSparkConfidenceHead.forward
  (model_executor/models/qwen3_dspark.py): torch.cat([h, e]).float(), then
  ReplicatedLinear(5376 -> 1, FP32 weight, no bias), whose
  UnquantizedLinearMethod under linear backend tilelang is F.linear, a cuBLAS
  SGEMM with one output column; then torch.sigmoid. Adaptive verification, on
  in the recipe, runs it on every draft step.
- Shapes: h is the drafter's head hidden (BF16, R x 5120), e the Markov
  embedding of the token before each draft (BF16, R x 256), w one FP32 row of
  5376; R = requests x 5 drafts. One stream: R = 5, which cuBLAS runs as dot
  products plus their reduction. Sixteen streams: R = 80, a GEMV; the steps
  with few requests take the dot path again.
- Computes: each draft's acceptance probability sigmoid(w . [h | e]), which
  rank 0 broadcasts; it sets the step's draft budget (running products of the
  confidences, ranked) and its dead rows (survival below 0.2).
- Batch dependence: cuBLAS picks dot or GEMV by R, so a draft's FP32 sum, and
  possibly its last bits, depend on how many requests the step drafts for.
  That can move a confidence across the budget's cut or the dead-row
  threshold, changing which drafts a step verifies and how many tokens it
  commits. It cannot change a temperature-0 token, which the target's
  verification decides.
- Around it: ATen's concatenation, FP32 copy and sigmoid, which the TileLang
  head folds into its launch (the stack of the five Markov embeddings stays).

Not ported: the Markov head's own lookup (markov_w1, an nn.Embedding: ATen's
index_select, five a step), which belongs with the ATen glue; and the TP = 1
path (F.embedding), which the deployment never runs.

The lookup

tilelang/embedding.py: vocab_embedding (a custom op with the arguments of
ops.vocab_parallel_embedding) is one launch. A block covers one token's 2 KiB of
row (five blocks a token at DS4.1's width), 128 threads each copying sixteen
bytes as 32-bit words, or writing zeros for an id the rank does not own, so any
dtype's bits copy exactly; the ids and table load on the coherent path, which
GB10 serves faster for a load whose address depends on the one before (as CED's
row copies measured). TileLangEmbeddingMethod is the unquantized method with
this lookup; it declares both id dtypes' kernels for kernel preparation.

VocabParallelEmbedding's fused lookup (forward and forward_reduce_scatter) now
asks its method: UnquantizedEmbeddingMethod.parallel_embedding runs
ops.vocab_parallel_embedding as before, so nothing changes until a model's
method supplies its own.

Tests (tests/kernels/test_deepseek_v41_tilelang_embedding.py, SM12x with
TileLang): bit-identical to the CUDA kernel on tables of random bit patterns
(NaN payloads and signed zeros included) for DS4.1's TP3 and TP4 shards at
decode and prefill token counts with int32 and int64 ids, ids owned by other
ranks, padding rows and ids past the vocabulary on both sides; LoRA's added rows
at TP2 to TP8; FP32, FP16 and FP8 rows; the ranks' partials sum to the full
lookup; a slice of the reduce-scatter buffer; the method against the unquantized
method's; no tokens; refused layouts; the declared calls.
